# build output
/build/

# tools/build-ovmf.sh's edk2 checkout - deliberately outside /build/ (see
# that script's header comment), but still not something to commit
/ovmf-build/

# module-level readmes (keep only the top-level README.md tracked)
**/README.md
!/README.md
# ...with two exceptions, added deliberately rather than by drift.
#
# tests/ is a directory of things whose whole purpose is to be read
# before they are changed: tests/README.md carries the mutation-score
# table and the rule it produced (a test that asserts an absence
# constrains almost nothing), and tests/corpus/README.md explains which
# files in there are corpus and which are reproducers - which is the
# difference between a file that may be deleted and one that must not be.
# Neither survives a fresh clone under the blanket rule above, and both
# are the kind of thing somebody needs on the day they add a test.
#
# If the convention is meant to hold absolutely, delete these two lines
# and the documentation with them; that is a decision, not an oversight.
!/tests/README.md
!/tests/corpus/README.md

# ---- test artifacts ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Almost everything the test tiers produce already lands under /build/
# and is covered above: the host-test binary, the coverage report and its
# HTML, the fuzz binaries, build/test-history.tsv, build/perf-history.tsv,
# build/flakes.tsv, and the disk image and log that tools/crash-test.sh
# keeps when a power cut leaves an inconsistent filesystem. The
# interactive suite writes its failure screendumps to /tmp entirely
# (LEANOS_INPUT_ARTIFACTS overrides it).
#
# What is left is the fuzz corpus, and it needs splitting rather than
# ignoring wholesale, because two different kinds of file live there.

# The corpus proper: inputs libFuzzer found interesting, named by content
# hash. Machine-generated, and *every* run adds more - a committed corpus
# means `make fuzz-run` dirties the tree with dozens of new files each
# time, which is exactly the noise this section exists to remove. The
# cost of ignoring them is that a campaign starts cold; that is thirty
# seconds of a thirty-minute nightly run.
/tests/corpus/*/*

# ...but NOT the reproducers. A file libFuzzer names crash-, leak-,
# timeout- or oom- is an input that once broke something, and checking one
# in is what turns a finding into a permanent regression test rather than
# an afternoon. `make fuzz-run` writes them straight here
# (-artifact_prefix), so a fuzzer that finds something produces exactly
# one new file in `git status` and it is the one worth keeping.
#
# Deliberately un-ignored, and the reason is visibility rather than
# safety from `git clean` - which would remove an uncommitted reproducer
# either way. The point is that an ignored file is one nobody is prompted
# to commit: it sits in a working tree until the tree is thrown away, and
# the bug it reproduces is found again the hard way.
!/tests/corpus/*/crash-*
!/tests/corpus/*/leak-*
!/tests/corpus/*/timeout-*
!/tests/corpus/*/oom-*

# Coverage instrumentation, for the case where it is ever run outside
# /build/ - llvm-cov and gcov both drop these beside the object files.
*.profraw
*.profdata
*.gcda
*.gcno

# macOS and editor debris, which this tree has been lucky to avoid so far
# and will not stay lucky about.
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
CLAUDE.md
